reply.body:
Thinking about it, wouldn't the following simpler implementation also do the job:
```java
public ResolvedJavaType ownerType() {
    ResolvedJavaType result = method.getDeclaringClass();
    while (result instanceof WrappedJavaType) {
        result = ((WrappedJavaType) result).getWrapped();
    }
    return result;
}

@olpaw Yes, the partial unwrapping is needed. In the case where we the wrapping is

    HostedType -> AnalysisType -> SubstitutionType

calling getWrapped().getWrapped() will result in a call to [AnnotationSubstitutionProcessor.resolve](https://github.com/oracle/graal/blob/85736d89e39a6a4fb700c45c1bd092205ad4ae3f/substratevm/src/com.oracle.svm.hosted/src/com/oracle/svm/hosted/substitute/AnnotationSubstitutionProcessor.java#L131) and return the annotated type.

> Thinking about it, wouldn't the following simpler implementation also do the job:
>
>     public ResolvedJavaType ownerType() {
>        ResolvedJavaType result = method.getDeclaringClass();
>        while (result instanceof WrappedJavaType) {
>            result = ((WrappedJavaType) result).getWrapped();
>        }
>        return result;
>     }

No, as noted above, that will return the annotated type when method.getDeclaringClass() is a SubstitutionType.
